cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use TerraFlow in your research, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "TerraFlow: A Reproducible Framework for Climate-Impact Assessment of Agricultural Suitability"
version: 0.5.0
date-released: "2026-06-30"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/gmarupilla/AgroTerraFlow"
url: "https://terraflow.marupilla.dev"
abstract: >
  TerraFlow is an open-source Python framework for reproducible
  climate-impact assessment of agricultural suitability. As of v0.5.x
  it provides a config-driven pipeline for raster ingestion, ROI
  clipping, spatial climate interpolation (linear, IDW, or Ordinary
  Kriging with LOOCV variogram selection) from weather-station CSV
  observations, suitability scoring with Monte-Carlo uncertainty
  propagation, global sensitivity analysis (Sobol' / Morris), spatial
  block cross-validation, and provenance-tracked artifact export.
  Every run produces a deterministic content-addressed fingerprint
  enabling independent verification of results. v0.5.0 adds
  climate-induced crop hazards (growing-degree days, frost days, heat
  stress days, precipitation percentiles, simplified Thornthwaite SPEI)
  under historical and projected future climate (CMIP6 NetCDF
  ingestion with SSP scenarios). The same workflow methodology extends
  to habitat suitability, land-use planning, and conservation siting.
keywords:
  - geospatial
  - agriculture
  - reproducibility
  - raster
  - climate
  - workflow
  - Python
  - kriging
  - sensitivity-analysis
  - uncertainty-quantification
